\section{It needs an Earthdata Login}{

This is the only source in this package that will not work until you have
created an account, and the failure without one is misleading: NASA answers
an unauthenticated request with HTTP 200 and the login page. Register once
at \url{https://urs.earthdata.nasa.gov/users/new}, generate an appkey at
\url{https://oceandata.sci.gsfc.nasa.gov/appkey/}, and put it in \verb{~/.Renviron}:

\if{html}{\out{<div class="sourceCode">}}\preformatted{EARTHDATA_APPKEY=your-key-here
}\if{html}{\out{</div>}}

A \verb{~/.netrc} entry for \code{urs.earthdata.nasa.gov} works too. See
\code{\link[=obdaac_credentials]{obdaac_credentials()}} for both routes and where they are looked for. A
download that comes back as the login page is refused by name rather than
being written to disk as a broken file.
}

\section{It downloads the whole globe}{

OB.DAAC serves Level-3 mapped fields as global static files with no
server-side subsetting, so \strong{one variable for one day is one global file}
however small the bounding box, and the subset is taken locally. At 4 km a
daily chlorophyll field is about 15 MB and at 9 km about 5, so \code{resolution = "9km"} is three times cheaper for a study whose grid is coarser than 4 km
anyway.

The exact total is reported before anything is transferred, because the file
search returns each file's size, and the extracted subsets are cached, so a
long record is paid for once.
}

\section{Why there is no eight-day option}{

OB.DAAC publishes eight-day composites and they are the obvious answer to
cloud gaps, but this package cannot join them honestly. \code{\link[=matchData]{matchData()}} joins
on an hour, a day, a month or a year, and an eight-day bin is none of those:
stamped as a day it would demand an observation fall on the bin's first
date, and nearly every row would go unmatched. Use \code{"monthly"}, which is a
step the join understands, or \code{\link[=fill_satellite_gaps]{fill_satellite_gaps()}} on the daily field,
which records what it filled.
}

\section{Which sensor, and why it matters}{

\code{sensor} defaults to \code{"MODISA"}, which has the longest current record with
both colour and SST. It is a choice you should make deliberately rather than
inherit — see the section of \code{\link[=obdaac_sensors]{obdaac_sensors()}} on why the sensors are not
interchangeable — and \code{\link[=source_of]{source_of()}} records which one answered.
}

